1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:08,184
The brush clears a silver coin, with lines of Arabic script. It was struck far from India, yet it is found here.

2
00:00:08,184 --> 00:00:15,325
From the eighth century, people, goods and ideas came to the subcontinent from the west, by sea and by the passes.

3
00:00:15,325 --> 00:00:19,401
Why do coins from far away turn up in an Indian field?

4
00:00:32,083 --> 00:00:41,122
How do we know? Arab and Ghaznavid coins survive. The Chachnama, a Persian text, tells the story of Sind.

5
00:00:41,122 --> 00:00:50,007
And Al-Biruni, a scholar, wrote a detailed book on India. Each source has its own point of view, so historians compare them.

6
00:01:01,217 --> 00:01:10,670
In 711 to 712 CE, an Arab army led by Muhammad bin Qasim entered Sind. Dahir, its ruler, was defeated.

7
00:01:10,670 --> 00:01:15,081
Arab rule stayed in Sind and Multan, and did not extend far.

8
00:01:15,081 --> 00:01:21,356
Arab merchants had long traded along the west coast, and settled in places such as Kerala and Gujarat.

9
00:01:21,356 --> 00:01:29,476
In the early eleventh century, Mahmud of Ghazni raided northern India many times, between 997 and 1030.

10
00:01:29,476 --> 00:01:36,452
The best-known raid was on Somnath, in 1025. His aim was wealth as well as power.

11
00:01:36,452 --> 00:01:40,806
With Mahmud came Al-Biruni, a scholar who learnt Sanskrit.

12
00:01:40,806 --> 00:01:48,252
His book, the Kitab-ul-Hind, describes Indian science, religion, society and customs, with fairness.

13
00:01:48,252 --> 00:01:59,139
In 1191, Prithviraj the Third defeated Muhammad of Ghor at Tarain. In 1192, Ghor won the second battle at Tarain.

14
00:01:59,139 --> 00:02:02,369
This opened the way for new rule in north India.

15
00:02:02,369 --> 00:02:08,981
Contact also meant new ideas, and the exchange of knowledge between India and the Arab world.

16
00:02:20,183 --> 00:02:24,172
Scholars disagree. How should we read these events?

17
00:02:24,172 --> 00:02:29,601
One view relies on the chroniclers of the time, who often described them in religious terms.

18
00:02:29,601 --> 00:02:38,746
Another view, held by many modern historians, stresses wealth, politics and trade as motives, and also the exchange of ideas.

19
00:02:38,746 --> 00:02:44,965
Most scholars treat the sources with care, since they were written by people on one side or the other.

20
00:02:56,150 --> 00:03:10,412
Carve this tip into memory. 711 Sind, 1025 Somnath. 1192 Tarain. Three dates in a row. Add Al-Biruni's book for the cultural side.

21
00:03:21,617 --> 00:03:39,103
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Who led the Arab army into Sind in 711? The answer: Muhammad bin Qasim. Number two.

22
00:03:39,103 --> 00:03:57,285
Who wrote the Kitab-ul-Hind? The answer: Al-Biruni. Number three. Where did Prithviraj and Muhammad of Ghor fight in 1191?

23
00:03:57,285 --> 00:04:08,822
The answer: Tarain. Now one line for Mains. Evaluate the impact of Arab and Turkish contacts before 1200 CE.

24
00:04:24,017 --> 00:04:31,390
The silver coin goes into its case. It is a small trace of a long contact. Three facts to take away.

25
00:04:31,390 --> 00:04:42,071
Silver dirham with Arabic script. Arabs reached Sind in 711 to 712 CE. Al-Biruni wrote on India in about 1030 CE.

26
00:04:42,071 --> 00:04:46,636
Next gallery: Land, Lords and Villages.

